<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Callao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Combate del Dos de Mayo · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands-war">Chincha Islands War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> was fought on 2 May 1866 between a Spanish naval squadron under Casto Méndez Núñez and the coastal fortifications of Callao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s main port, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands-war">Chincha Islands War</a>. After a day-long bombardment both sides claimed victory, but the Spanish fleet soon withdrew from the Pacific. The battle is celebrated in Peru as the Combate del Dos de Mayo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-callao.jpg" alt="Battle of Callao" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CombateDosdeMayo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, seeking to recover prestige in its former American colonies, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s guano-rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands">Chincha Islands</a> and demanded an indemnity for the murder of two Spanish citizens in Lambayeque. President Juan Antonio Pezet, judging Peru's navy too weak, began handing over guano, and in November 1865 Colonel Mariano Ignacio Prado took power in a coup and organised resistance. After the indecisive Battle of Abtao in February 1866, Méndez Núñez bombarded the undefended Chilean port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> and then turned on the well-defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, arriving on 25 April with seven warships and seven auxiliaries carrying 252 guns, led by the ironclad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numancia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>'s forts had been reinforced with five British-made 22-ton Blakely rifles and four Armstrong guns in two armoured turrets, Junín and La Merced; in all the Peruvians had 52 guns ashore, 13 more on warships, and two locally built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">ironclads</a>, Loa and Victoria. American, British and French ships watched. The Spanish entered the bay at 10:00 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numancia</a> opened fire at 11:50. A shot struck Numancia and wounded Núñez, but her armour held. A Blakely shell wrecked the boilers of Villa de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, which had to be towed away, and an Armstrong shot pierced Berenguela at the waterline.</p><p>A shot from Blanca destroyed La Merced, killing or wounding 93 men, among them the Minister of War and Navy, José Gálvez Egúsquiza. Other batteries were silenced, and many of the 3,000 Peruvian troops entrenched outside the forts were hit. By 17:00 Santa Rosa had three guns left firing, and the badly wounded Núñez ordered the fleet to cease fire. The damaged ships spent several days at San Lorenzo island tending their wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the battle was hailed as a victory; Núñez received the Grand Cross of the Order of Carlos III and was promoted to Lieutenant General, and before the Battle of Lissa the Austrian admiral Wilhelm von Tegetthoff urged his crews to imitate the Spanish at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>. Peruvians, for their part, counted it a victory, the day they stopped Spain from recolonising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, and the American eyewitness T. H. Nelson wrote that the damage to the town was barely noticeable. It was the last battle of the war between Spain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>; the Spanish expedition ended officially in 1868, but peace was not signed until 1879. A square and a metro station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> bear the name Callao.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Callao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Guano wealth bought a modern navy, and in 1866 Peru, allied with Chile against Spain, repelled a Spanish squadron at the <b>Battle of Callao</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
